## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design end-to-end trust from image provenance to runtime policy enforcement, plus canary control and rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Content Trust/Notary for image provenance
- SPIFFE/SVIDs via Vault for mTLS
- OPA policies loaded via Docker Config
- Sigver sidecar for per-request validation
- Canary rollout with latency/error thresholds and rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo example: verify image signature before starting app
async function verifySignature(image) {
  const sig = await notary.verify(image);
  if (!sig || !sig.valid) throw new Error('invalid image');
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle signature revocation and key rotation in this setup?
- How would you scale the sigver and policy sidecars across nodes?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design edge routing with policy-driven gating and cross-DC deployment, plus observability and security concerns in docker-dca.

## Key Concepts

- TLS termination at the edge with Nginx
- Central policy engine (OPA) integration
- Per-tenant feature flag evaluation
- Cross-DC routing semantics
- Observability with Prometheus and OTEL

## Code Example

```rego
package featureflag.allow
default allow = false
allow {
  input.tenant == "tenant-A"
  input.flag == "new_feature"
}
```

```nginx
# Nginx map-based routing example
http {
  map $http_x_feature_flag $upstream {
    default http://api_v1;
    "new_feature" http://api_v2;
  }
  server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    location /api/ { proxy_pass $upstream; }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement cache invalidation for policy decisions?
- How would you test the canary routing?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical skills in edge proxying, per-tenant isolation, simple sidecar patterns, and in-memory caching with TTL. It also exercises docker-compose bootstrapping, TLS termination, and cache invalidation on 5xx.

## Key Concepts
- In-memory TTL cache and eviction
- Per-tenant cache keys and header reliance
- Sidecar design that intercepts requests
- TLS termination at an edge proxy and routing by tenant
- Cache invalidation on error responses

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple in-memory TTL cache for GET responses
const cache = new Map();
function cacheMiddleware(req, res, next) {
  if (req.method !== 'GET') return next();
  const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant'] || 'default';
  const key = `${tenant}|${req.originalUrl}`;
  const now = Date.now();
  const entry = cache.get(key);
  if (entry && now < entry.expires) {
    res.set(entry.headers);
    return res.end(entry.body);
  }
  const origSend = res.send.bind(res);
  res.send = (body) => {
    cache.set(key, { body, expires: now + 60000, headers: res.getHeaders() });
    return origSend(body);
  };
  next();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this cache across multiple instances?
- How would you protect against unbounded memory growth?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests secure, rotating certs in Swarm with Vault, handling cert storage (secret vs volume), and canary semantics.

## Key Concepts
- Swarm multi-node with TLS and mTLS
- Vault PKI for dynamic certs
- Certs via writable volume + sidecar refresh
- Canary rollout with health checks and rollback
- Nginx TLS termination and service-to-service mTLS

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple cert refresh watcher from Vault to a mounted volume
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
const fs = require('fs');
async function refresh() {
  const res = await fetch('https://vault.example/v1/pki/issue/api', { headers: { 'X-Vault-Token': process.env.VAULT_TOKEN } });
  const cert = await res.text();
  fs.writeFileSync('/certs/api.crt', cert);
}
setInterval(refresh, 5 * 60 * 1000);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate cert rotation does not break existing connections?
- How would you monitor Vault token renewal and automatic revocation?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to enforce data residency and leakage controls at runtime with low impact, using Vault for policy and identity, and blue/green rollouts for safe deployments.

## Key Concepts
- Data-loss prevention in containerized traffic
- Vault integration and SPIFFE SVID rotation
- Tenant-scoped JSON schemas
- Dynamic policy updates and hot-reload
- Canary/Blue-green deployment patterns

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal pseudo sidecar validator
const Validator = require('jsonschema').Validator;
const v = new Validator();
const tenant = process.env.TENANT_HEADER; // from request header
const schema = fetchVaultSchema(tenant); // resolves /secret/tenants/{tenant}/schema
v.addSchema(schema, '/tenant/schema');
const payload = getOutboundBody();
const result = v.validate(payload, { // simplified schema
  type: 'object', properties: { tenant: {type: 'string'}, data: {type: 'object'} }, required: ['tenant','data']
}, '/tenant/schema');
if (!result.valid) blockOrRedact(payload, result.errors);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test false positives/negatives per tenant?
- How would you handle schema evolution without breaking existing tenants?
